      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A(x,y,z)=⟨3{e}^{-{z}^{2}},{x}^{3}y,1-{y}^{3}⟩$\n- **II.** $W(x,y)=⟨x-3{y}^{2},2-y⟩$\n- **III.** $C(x,y,z)=(x+2){e}^{yz}$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $W(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to values in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
