    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b765b037-cc49-500c-988d-b447495de80f",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Q(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$\n- **II.** $T(t)={e}^{t}\\,i-t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$\n- **III.** $D(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $T(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $D(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "0045645c-a237-52ea-950c-3e5387303b29",
